I found the bug! Look at the `srcLength >= 0` case in `u_strToUTF8WithSub`. In the fast loop:

```c
count = (int32_t)((pDestLimit - pDest) / 3);
srcLength = (int32_t)(pSrcLimit - pSrc);
if(count > srcLength) {
    count = srcLength;
}
if(count < 3) {
    break;
}
do {
    ch=*pSrc++;
    if(ch <= 0x7f) {
        *pDest++ = (uint8_t)ch;
    } else if(ch <= 0x7ff) {
        *pDest++=(uint8_t)((ch>>6)|0xc0);
        *pDest++=(uint8_t)((ch&0x3f)|0x80);
    } else if(ch <= 0xd7ff || ch >= 0xe000) {
        *pDest++=(uint8_t)((ch>>12)|0xe0);
        *pDest++=(uint8_t)(((ch>>6)&0x3f)|0x80);
        *pDest++=(uint8_t)((ch&0x3f)|0x80);
    } else /* ch is a surrogate */ {
        ...
    }
} while(--count > 0);
```

The fast loop computes `count = (pDestLimit - pDest) / 3`. This is the number of iterations where each iteration writes at most 3 bytes. But for ASCII chars (`ch <= 0x7f`), each iteration writes only 1 byte. So the loop can write at most `count * 3` bytes, which is `<= pDestLimit - pDest`. So no overflow there.

Wait, but the surrogate case writes 4 bytes. Let me re-read:

```c
